  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1... \nHigh pressure will build in behind the cold front this evening and \ntonight with strong pressure rises and northerly winds gusting 15-\n25mph. Canadian airmass settles in for Monday with dewpoints down \ninto the lower 50s and daytime high temperatures that will struggle \nto reach 70F. Monday night lows could drop into the upper 40s form \nthe higher elevations in the south-central portion of the CWA \nnortheastward into northwest PA. This cool spell for the southern \nGreat Lakes will be brief, however, as the high pressure system \nwill be transient and will shift eastward fairly quickly with return \nflow coming into play Tuesday. Dewpoints and temperatures again on \nthe increase and could see highs 10-15 degrees warmer than Monday. \nMeanwhile, a warm front will be stretching eastward into the \nvicinity of the lengthwise fetch of Lake Erie, introducing POPs into \nour zones for Tuesday night. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... \nThe warm front will become quasi-stationary over the CWA from \nTuesday night through Thursday night with dewpoints back in the \nupper 60s to lower 70s and air temperatures in the low to mid 80s. \nWill need to examine some of the convective indices as we get closer \nto mid week for thunderstorm and any possible severe potential, but \nexpecting an extended period of on/off convection in more summer-\nlike weather as we approach the solstice. Frontal system should \nclear the area after Thursday as a cold front with modest \ntemperatures falls for Friday.",
